Drop the old kqchan fd on a second knote create, or two epoll entries leak per knote.

Pass 44 and 0004 fixed the delete path, but the guest still burned a core in epoll_pwait after ciderd
exited. /proc/PID/fdinfo of the survivor's epoll told the truth: TWO different sockets registered in
the same epoll with the SAME data pointer -- one knote, two descriptors. knote_create is the only code
that ever assigns kn_dupfd, so a second create on the same (calloc'd) knote overwrote kn_dupfd and left
the first descriptor still registered, its data.ptr pointing at this knote. Nothing removed it; once the
knote was freed epoll reported that descriptor forever, linux_kevent_copyout saw a zero-filter knote and
discarded it, and the caller spun with no recv -- the strace we captured.

Before assigning a new kn_dupfd, drop any descriptor the knote already holds: EPOLL_CTL_DEL it and close
it. A fresh knote has kn_dupfd 0 (calloc) so this is a no-op on first create; it only fires on the
re-create that would otherwise orphan a descriptor (NOTE_TRACK hands us a fresh kqchan per fork).
---
--- a/src/linux/machport.c
+++ b/src/linux/machport.c
@@ -206,6 +206,19 @@
     kn->data.events = EPOLLIN;
     kn->kn_epollfd = filter_epfd(filt);
 
+    /*
+     * A second create on the same knote (calloc'd, so kn_dupfd starts 0) would overwrite kn_dupfd
+     * below and ORPHAN the previous descriptor in the epoll set: it stays registered with
+     * data.ptr = this knote and nothing ever removes it, so once the knote is freed epoll reports
+     * it forever -- the #15 teardown spin, observed as two epoll entries sharing one knote pointer.
+     * Drop the old registration first.
+     */
+    if (kn->kdata.kn_dupfd > 0) {
+        (void) epoll_ctl(kn->kn_epollfd, EPOLL_CTL_DEL, kn->kdata.kn_dupfd, NULL);
+        (void) __close_for_kqueue(kn->kdata.kn_dupfd);
+        kn->kdata.kn_dupfd = -1;
+    }
+
     memset(&ev, 0, sizeof(ev));
     ev.events = kn->data.events;
     ev.data.ptr = kn;
--- a/src/linux/proc.c
+++ b/src/linux/proc.c
@@ -183,6 +183,20 @@
     kn->data.events = EPOLLIN;
     kn->kn_epollfd = filter_epfd(filt);
 
+    /*
+     * A second create on the same knote (calloc'd, so kn_dupfd starts 0) would overwrite kn_dupfd
+     * below and ORPHAN the previous descriptor in the epoll set: it stays registered with
+     * data.ptr = this knote and nothing ever removes it, so once the knote is freed epoll reports
+     * it forever -- the #15 teardown spin, observed as two epoll entries sharing one knote pointer.
+     * Drop the old registration first. (With NOTE_TRACK, the server hands us a fresh kqchan fd per
+     * fork; a re-create that reuses the knote is exactly how the orphan happens.)
+     */
+    if (kn->kdata.kn_dupfd > 0) {
+        (void) epoll_ctl(kn->kn_epollfd, EPOLL_CTL_DEL, kn->kdata.kn_dupfd, NULL);
+        (void) __close_for_kqueue(kn->kdata.kn_dupfd);
+        kn->kdata.kn_dupfd = -1;
+    }
+
     memset(&ev, 0, sizeof(ev));
     ev.events = kn->data.events;
     ev.data.ptr = kn;
